export const BEST_PRACTICES = "# Resume Best Practices\n\n## The Core Idea\n\nRecruiters spend ~6 seconds on a first pass. Your resume is a filter, not a biography. Every word either earns its place or gets cut.\n\n## Format Rules\n\n- **One page.** Two pages only if 10+ years of directly relevant experience.\n- Reverse chronological. Most recent job first, always.\n- No photos, no \"Objective\" sections, no references line.\n- Margins: 0.5–0.75 inch. Font: 10–11pt. Serif or clean sans-serif (Garamond, Calibri, Arial).\n- Consistent formatting. If one role uses bold for company name, all do.\n- PDF only. Never submit a .docx unless explicitly asked.\n\n## Sections (in order)\n\n1. **Name + Contact** — name (large), email, phone, LinkedIn, GitHub, city/state (no full address)\n2. **Summary** — optional, 2–3 lines max, only if it adds real context (career switch, niche specialization). Skip it if it's generic.\n3. **Skills** — group by type: Languages, Frameworks, Tools, etc. Don't list things you can't talk about.\n4. **Experience** — most of the resume. See bullet rules below.\n5. **Projects** — 2–4 relevant projects. Include tech stack and a link if live.\n6. **Education** — school, degree, graduation year. GPA only if 3.5+ and < 3 years out of school.\n\n## Bullet Rules (Most Important)\n\n**Formula: [Action verb] + [what you did] + [measurable result or scope]**\n\nGood: \"Reduced API latency by 40% by migrating from REST polling to WebSocket streaming\"\nBad: \"Responsible for improving API performance\"\n\n- Start every bullet with a strong past-tense verb: Built, Designed, Reduced, Automated, Led, Shipped, Migrated, Refactored, Deployed, Optimized\n- Quantify wherever possible: %, $, users, requests/sec, time saved, team size\n- 3–5 bullets per role. No more.\n- Each bullet = one idea. Not a run-on sentence with three ideas stapled together.\n- Skip \"soft skill\" bullets (\"Collaborated with cross-functional teams\") unless you're explicitly leading or managing.\n\n## ATS / Keyword Matching\n\n- Mirror exact phrases from the JD. If they say \"distributed systems\", use \"distributed systems\", not \"scalable architecture\".\n- Put keywords naturally in bullet text, not just in a keyword dump at the bottom.\n- Don't fake skills. ATS gets you the interview; the interviewer exposes you.\n\n## Tailoring for a Specific Role\n\n1. Read the JD. Identify: required skills, preferred skills, domain, company size/stage.\n2. Reorder bullets within each role to front-load the most relevant work.\n3. Swap generic verbs for domain-specific ones (\"Deployed ML model to prod\" vs \"Built feature\").\n4. If a project isn't relevant to this role, cut it or shrink it to one line.\n5. Summary (if used) should reference the company's domain or stack directly.\n6. Check: does every bullet pass the \"so what?\" test? If the impact isn't clear, add it or cut the bullet.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- Listing responsibilities instead of achievements\n- Using \"I\" anywhere\n- Passive voice (\"was responsible for\", \"helped to\")\n- Buzzword stuffing without substance (\"synergized cross-functional stakeholders\")\n- Inconsistent tense (current job: present tense; past jobs: past tense)\n- Contact info missing or outdated\n- Skills section listing things you barely touched 3 years ago\n";
